<body>
  <h1>Asyncio best practices</h1>
  <p>Asyncio is a powerful library for asynchronous programming in Python, but it’s easy to misuse it if you’re not familiar with its idioms. Here are some widely‑agreed‑upon best‑practice recommendations:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Prefer <code>async with</code> and <code>async for</code> over manual <code>await</code> loops.</strong>  These constructs automatically handle resource cleanup and are more readable.</li>
    <li><strong>Never block the event loop.</strong>  Use <code>await asyncio.sleep(0)</code> or run CPU‑bound code in a thread‑pool/executor instead of long‑running synchronous calls.</li>
    <li><strong>Structure your program around a single top‑level <code>asyncio.run()</code> call.</strong>  Avoid nesting event loops or calling <code>loop.run_until_complete()</code> inside coroutines.</li>
    <li><strong>Use <code>asyncio.create_task()</code> for fire‑and‑forget background jobs.</strong>  Keep a reference to the task if you need to await its result or handle exceptions.</li>
    <li><strong>Cancel tasks cleanly.</strong>  Propagate <code>asyncio.CancelledError</code> and give tasks a chance to finish cleanup before exiting.</li>
    <li><strong>Leverage built‑in synchronization primitives (queues, locks, events) instead of custom polling.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Limit concurrency with semaphores or <code>asyncio.gather(..., return_exceptions=True)</code> to avoid overwhelming external services.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Document the expected concurrency model.</strong>  Make it clear whether functions are “safe to run concurrently” or require serialization.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For a deeper dive, see the official Python documentation and the “async‑timeout” and “aiomultiprocess” libraries for common patterns.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

In short, the page provides a concise numbered list of the most common asyncio best‑practice recommendations (use `async with`/`async for`, never block the event loop, keep a single `asyncio.run()`, create background tasks with `asyncio.create_task()`, cancel tasks cleanly, prefer built‑in synchronization primitives, limit concurrency, and clearly document concurrency expectations).

